//! `sos`: the SOS userspace API (spec.md §2, §5.7, §12).
//!
//! User-mode library code, owned and exported by the kernel package. Nothing
//! here runs in kernel mode; all of it lives in the kernel's tree. This is the
//! vDSO discipline, and it buys one thing: syscall numbers are not ABI. The op
//! numbers come from `sosabi`, which the kernel's dispatch tables import too,
//! so the two halves of the contract cannot skew, and because a process links
//! this module instead of writing numbers, the kernel may renumber between
//! builds without breaking anything compiled against it.
//!
//! Three entry altitudes, one implementation chain. Exactly one place in the
//! system issues a trap, and everything above it is a wrapper:
//!
//!   typed Saw   `system.shutdown(0)`                 <- prefer this
//!   typed C     `sos_system_shutdown(h, 0)`          <- for non-Saw languages
//!   raw         `sos_syscall1(h, op, arg)`           <- the HAL stub
//!
//! The Saw methods below call the `@export`ed C functions, which call the raw
//! form, which is the per-arch `ecall`/`svc` stub in `hal/<arch>/user/`. No
//! altitude reimplements the one below it, so there is no second trap path to
//! keep in step.
//!
//! What a process must still agree with the kernel about: the calling
//! convention (§5.7: handle in a0, op in a7, status back in a0) and the status
//! tags it branches on. The first lives in the HAL stub; the second is
//! `SosStatus`. Neither is a number this file's callers write.
//!
//! The two layers, and which one carries a guarantee. The typed Saw surface
//! never exposes or accepts a raw handle word: every wrapper keeps its handle
//! field private or `public(package)`, offers no word extractor and no public
//! word constructor, and is minted inside this package by the op wrapper that
//! received it from the kernel. The only crossing into the typed layer is
//! `System(boot_handle:)` (§12's root of trust), and there is no crossing out.
//! A handle you hold is one the kernel handed you, and a handle you were never
//! given is not a thing you can spell. The `@export`ed C surface below is the
//! floor and keeps raw `UInt` handles, because a C caller has no type system to
//! carry the guarantee: a non-Saw process gets the same operations without the
//! protection, and a process that wants the protection writes Saw.
//!
//! Which enums are public API and which are bookkeeping. The vDSO discipline is
//! about op numbers, not argument encodings, and the line runs exactly there:
//!
//!   Public: an enum or record whose values a process chooses and passes as a
//!   syscall argument, or that the kernel hands back as data. `EventMode` is
//!   the first kind: a caller names it (`event_create(mode:
//!   EventMode.SaturatingSum)`), and a method per mode stops scaling once an
//!   object has several modes. `BootHandleKind` and the pipe levels are the
//!   second kind: the kernel writes them and userspace matches on them, so
//!   one declaration has to serve both. The wait record is decoded inside this
//!   package, so the facade re-exports the decoded `WaitResult` and
//!   `WaitPayload` (from `sos.pipe`, because they hold owning wrappers) rather
//!   than `WaitTag` and the record's word offsets.
//!
//!   Kernel-internal: op numbers, the rights enums not re-exported below,
//!   `ObjType`, `FaultReason`, the dispatch's bookkeeping. A process never
//!   writes one of these, which leaves the kernel free to renumber between
//!   builds.
//!
//! Either way there is exactly one declaration, and for a public one it stays
//! in `sosabi`: the kernel's dispatch decodes the same enum, and a second copy
//! here is the skew that file exists to prevent. A `public import`
//! re-exports, so the one declaration is reachable through this module and a
//! process writes `import sos.{EventMode}`. Only the names a `public import`
//! below lists are reachable through `sos`, but `sosabi` is on a process's
//! module path as a transitive dependency regardless, so the split is held by
//! what userspace is told to write rather than by a wall. Don't move the enum
//! into this module for the kernel to import: this module and the kernel HAL
//! export the two runtime hooks under the same C names, so importing one into
//! the other is a duplicate-`@export` error.
//!
//! # This file is the facade
//!
//! It holds no code. It re-exports the names a process writes, so `import
//! sos.{System}` means the same thing wherever it appears. Each object lives in
//! its own module.
//!
//! The modules, in dependency order. A file may import only modules listed
//! before it, because an import cycle is a compile error naming the loop:
//!
//!   `floor`      the two syscall externs, the status split, the whole
//!                `@export`ed C-ABI surface, `ALL_RIGHTS`, the owning tier's
//!                contract, and the boot handle the runtime hooks print
//!                through
//!   `memory`     Memory: an allocation of RAM, and `split`
//!   `pool`       Pool: RAM not yet allocated, `allocate` and `available`
//!   `iomemory`   IoMemory: device authority, and `carve`
//!   `mapping`    Mapping: one installed protection row, and `unmap`
//!   `thread`     Thread
//!   `event`      Event
//!   `interrupt`  Interrupt
//!   `timer`      Timer
//!   `clock`      Clock, which constructs Timer
//!   `waiter`     Waiter, whose `add` overloads name Event, Interrupt and
//!                Timer
//!   `pipe`       PipeInlet + PipeOutlet (the two ends of a connection, and
//!                `post` / `take`) and PipeReply + PipeRequest (the one-shot
//!                pair one exchange is made of, and `resolve` / `reply`).
//!                Pipe types, alone among these, share a file because they are
//!                two pairs and every sentence about either half of a pair
//!                explains it by contrast with its opposite. It comes after
//!                `waiter` because it extends `Waiter` with the pipe overloads
//!   `system`     System, Process and BootHandle; see below
//!   `rt`         `sos_rt_write` / `sos_rt_abort`, both written over a System
//!                op
//!
//! Where the line fell:
//!
//!   - `waiter` comes after `event`, `interrupt` and `timer`. `add` is one
//!     overload per kind and each overload reads the target's handle field, so
//!     `waiter` imports those three and none of them imports it.
//!   - `system` holds three types, because every pair of them names the other:
//!     System constructs Process, `Process.give(system:)` consumes a System,
//!     and `BootHandle` carries all three while `Process` answers with one.
//!     See `system.saw`'s header. There is no `process.saw`.
//!   - The map funnels are `Process.map(memory:access:)` and
//!     `Process.map(iomemory:)`, not `Memory.map(into: &Process)`: `BootHandle`
//!     carries a `Memory?` and an `IoMemory?`, so `system` imports both of
//!     those modules, and a `map` written in either naming `Process` is an
//!     import cycle. The region is borrowed rather than consumed, because a map
//!     moves no handle word.
//!   - The `Waiter` overloads for kinds declared after `waiter` live with
//!     those kinds as `extension Waiter`: the pipe ends and one-shots in
//!     `pipe.saw`, `Waiter.add(process:, key:)` in `system.saw`. Extension
//!     lookup follows `public import`, so each call site reads as a member of
//!     `waiter.saw`'s overload list. The raw attach funnel in `waiter.saw` is
//!     `public(package)` so those extensions can reach it.
//!
//! What a split costs: the names two seams share are `public(package)`.
//! `kernel/sysapi/Saw.toml` is a manifest root, so it is a real package and the
//! tier is a real package-internal one. The wrapper handle fields a sibling
//! constructs or reads (Memory, IoMemory, Mapping, Thread, Event, Interrupt,
//! Timer, Clock, Waiter, and the pipe types) are reachable by their siblings
//! and by nothing outside the package. `System`'s and `Process`'s stay private
//! because only their own module touches them. Nothing is widened to bare
//! `public`.

// The encodings a process is meant to write or match on are the only things
// this module re-exports from `sosabi`. This line carries the status tag every
// `Result<_, SosStatus>` hands back, the mode an `event_create(mode:)` caller
// names, the domain a `clock_get(type:)` caller names, the kind a boot-set
// record reports, and the pipe levels: `OutletLevel` and `InletLevel` (what
// the pipe `WaitPayload` cases carry) and `ReplyLevel` (the discriminator the
// reply decode reads). The kernel writes each level and userspace reads it,
// and a process cannot match on a payload whose sub-enum it cannot name.
// `import sos.{SosStatus}` (etc.) is how a process takes them.
public import sosabi.{SosStatus, EventMode, ClockType,
                      BootHandleKind, OutletLevel, InletLevel, ReplyLevel}

// `MapAccess` is public because `Process.map` takes it: a value a process
// chooses and passes as a syscall argument. The memory type is a kind
// (`IoMemory`), not an argument, so `map(iomemory:)` takes no mode at all.
public import sosabi.{MapAccess}

// `SlabKind` is public because `System.slab_donate` takes it: a value a process
// chooses and passes as a syscall argument. It is the one piece of the slab
// machinery that crosses the wall. The extents, floors and capacities stay
// kernel-internal, because a donor names which slab it is feeding and never how
// big the slab is.
public import sosabi.{SlabKind}

// `ProtectionTier` is public because `System.tier` hands it back: the other
// clause of the rule, a value the kernel hands back as data (like
// `BootHandleKind` and `OutletLevel`). A process cannot `match` on a tier whose
// cases it cannot name, and matching is the intended use: read it once, and
// refuse to run if you needed the other word.
//
// This does not breach the op-number wall: what is republished is a name, and
// what number it carries is the build's business.
public import sosabi.{ProtectionTier}

// `HandleInfo` and `HandleKind` are public for the floor's `sos_handle_info`:
// the selector is a value a process chooses and passes as a syscall argument,
// and the kind is a value the kernel hands back as data. The typed tier needs
// neither (each wrapper's `rights()` knows its own kind and selector), so a
// Saw process meets them only when it calls the floor directly.
public import sosabi.{HandleInfo, HandleKind}

// Every kind's rights enum is public. A holder narrows any handle it owns at a
// mint (`mint(rights:)`, on each wrapper whose default set carries `Mint`), at
// a give's keep mask or before a send, and each of those takes a keep mask the
// caller assembles out of these bits: values a process chooses and passes as a
// syscall argument. A kind's default set is permissive, because attenuation is
// monotonic and a bit not minted at creation could never appear later, so the
// policy is a mask written where a handle is handed on, and it can only be
// spelled with the enum.
//
// This does not breach the op-number wall: userspace never writes an op, and a
// right is a capability rather than an index the kernel trusts. The enums
// publish names, and what number a name carries is the build's business.
//
// Some of what the masks buy:
//
// - `SystemRight`: `System.mint(rights:)` is how a launcher decides what a
//   child may ask its System object for.
// - `MemoryRight.MapExecute` is what makes narrowing a region worth doing:
//   withholding the authority to map executable is the first policy anybody
//   writes about RAM (`Split` and `Map` are what a region is for, so a launcher
//   had no other reason to narrow it). `PoolRight` carries the same bit at the
//   same number, so the policy can be written once on a pool and binds
//   everything allocated through it.
// - `ProcessRight.Stats` is a bit whose whole point is to be withheld. "You may
//   learn my child died, and you may not watch it work" is a policy a
//   supervisor states once at a mint.
// - `EventRight` masks a `Process.give(event:, keep:)`. A giver that attaches
//   the Event to a Waiter itself withholds `Wait`, since an object is attached
//   to at most one Waiter whichever handle names it; a producer end is
//   `Signal` alone.
// - The pipe rights are where a launcher's pipe policy is written: "this child
//   may post and may not hand the end on" is a mask at the mint and nothing
//   else. `PipeRequest.mint(rights:)` through a mask of `Transfer | Mint`
//   describes a courier, a delegate that may pass an obligation further along
//   §2.1's forwarding chain and may never speak for it.
public import sosabi.{SystemRight, ProcessRight, ThreadRight, EventRight,
                      WaiterRight, InterruptRight, ClockRight, TimerRight,
                      MemoryRight, PoolRight, IoMemoryRight, MappingRight,
                      PipeInletRight, PipeOutletRight, PipeReplyRight,
                      PipeRequestRight}

// `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` is a size the two halves must agree on. A `post` may not
// name more, a delivered message's body region is that wide, and the typed
// methods take a `&[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES]`, so a caller cannot declare the
// buffer without the number. It publishes no op and gates no authority; it is
// the same kind of thing `wait_record_bytes()` is. The staging ring's depth
// stays in `kcore.limits`: a caller meeting a full ring is told `WouldBlock`
// without needing to know how deep it was.
public import sosabi.{PIPE_BODY_BYTES, PIPE_MSG_HANDLES}

// Nothing else in `sosabi` is re-exported: not the op numbers, the handle
// aliases, or `WaitTag` and the wait record's word offsets. The record's
// layout is still a contract with C callers (see `sos_waiter_wait`); a Saw
// caller reads it decoded as a `WaitResult`. The modules below import what
// each of them needs directly with ordinary imports, so a process cannot reach
// one through `sos` under any spelling.

// The object modules, and the C-ABI floor under them. Re-exported by name
// rather than by glob: the list is this module's surface, so a name that is not
// here is not something a process can reach, and adding one is a decision with
// a line to write.
public import sos.floor.{ALL_RIGHTS, sos_handle_mint, sos_handle_info,
                         sos_handle_release,
                         sos_system_debug_print, sos_system_shutdown,
                         sos_system_process_self, sos_system_process_create,
                         sos_system_clock_get, sos_system_slab_donate,
                         sos_system_interrupt_bind,
                         sos_system_process_next, sos_system_process_by_pid,
                         sos_process_thread_create, sos_process_thread_self,
                         sos_process_exit, sos_process_get_status,
                         sos_process_kill, sos_process_stats,
                         sos_process_event_create, sos_process_waiter_create,
                         sos_process_start,
                         sos_process_give, sos_process_boot_handle_next,
                         sos_process_restrict_rights, sos_process_ceiling,
                         sos_thread_start, sos_thread_join, sos_thread_exit,
                         sos_thread_yield,
                         sos_event_signal, sos_event_receive,
                         sos_waiter_add, sos_waiter_remove, sos_waiter_wait,
                         sos_interrupt_ack,
                         sos_clock_now, sos_clock_timer_create,
                         sos_timer_arm, sos_timer_disarm,
                         sos_pool_allocate, sos_pool_available,
                         sos_memory_split, sos_memory_map,
                         sos_iomemory_carve, sos_iomemory_map,
                         sos_mapping_unmap,
                         sos_process_pipe_create, sos_pipe_inlet_post,
                         sos_pipe_inlet_call,
                         sos_pipe_outlet_take, sos_pipe_reply_resolve,
                         sos_pipe_reply_ready,
                         sos_pipe_request_reply, sos_pipe_request_reply_wait,
                         sos_pipe_request_sender}
public import sos.memory.{Memory}
public import sos.pool.{Pool, PoolAvailable}
public import sos.iomemory.{IoMemory}
public import sos.mapping.{Mapping}
public import sos.pipe.{PipeInlet, PipeOutlet, PipeReply, PipeRequest,
                        PipeHandle, PipeHandles, PipeMsg,
                        WaitResult, WaitPayload, ReplyDelivery, ReplyWaitError}
public import sos.thread.{Thread}
public import sos.event.{Event}
public import sos.interrupt.{Interrupt}
public import sos.timer.{Timer}
public import sos.clock.{Clock}
public import sos.waiter.{AttachMode, Waiter}
// `ProcessStats` is not a capability: it is the answer `Process.stats()` gives,
// three plain counts and a pid a process may hold, copy and print. A name on
// this line is a name a process can reach, and the reading has to be one: a
// method whose return type could not be spelled is a method nobody can bind.
public import sos.system.{System, Process, ProcessStats, BootHandle, StatsRegion}

// The one non-re-exporting line, for a reason no `public import` can serve:
// `sos.rt` publishes nothing (its two functions are module-private and reach
// the world through `@export` alone), so the facade has no name to re-export
// and still has to name the module, because an import is what compiles it into
// the unit. Without this line a process links with no `sos_rt_write` and its
// first `print` goes nowhere.
import sos.rt
